  "problem": "Given that\n$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}],$\nfind ${2}^{D}.$\n\n| ${2}^{D}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |",
  "worked_solution": "Given a function $f$ and a diagonal matrix $D$ with diagonal entries ${\\lambda }_{1},\u2026,{\\lambda }_{n},$ we have\n\n$$\nf(D)=[\\begin{aligned}f({\\lambda }_{1}) & \u22ef & 0 \\\\ \u22ee & \u22f1 & \u22ee \\\\ 0 & \u22ef & f({\\lambda }_{n})\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{D} & =[\\begin{aligned}{2}^{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{-2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
